WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.882
In data structures

00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:05.130
the facts we care about are statements

00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:06.712
about every input.

00:00:06.795 --> 00:00:09.710
Every binary tree with n leaves has

00:00:09.793 --> 00:00:12.041
n - 1 internal nodes.

00:00:12.041 --> 00:00:13.957
This loop always ends.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.455
This algorithm always returns

00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:18.620
the k-th largest element.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.957
Trying a few cases cannot

00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:24.375
settle such statements, so we need proofs

00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:26.363
and the slides use four.

00:00:26.363 --> 00:00:29.145
To show that a statement is false

00:00:29.145 --> 00:00:30.576
a counterexample.

00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:33.755
To show that it is true: contradiction.

00:00:33.835 --> 00:00:36.299
To show that it holds for every

00:00:36.378 --> 00:00:39.399
n from some starting value: induction.

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:42.181
And for every recursive structure

00:00:42.181 --> 00:00:43.930
structural induction.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.773
To show that a claim of the

00:00:46.856 --> 00:00:48.867
form 'for all x' is false

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:52.052
one example where it fails is enough

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.560
a counterexample.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.236
All primes are odd?

00:00:55.236 --> 00:00:57.750
False: 2 is prime and even.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.732
A better example uses the Fibonacci numbers

00:01:01.732 --> 00:01:04.704
numbered from 1 as on the slides

00:01:04.704 --> 00:01:07.263
F(0) and F(1) are both 1

00:01:07.345 --> 00:01:09.574
and each next number is the

00:01:09.656 --> 00:01:11.472
sum of the two before.

00:01:11.472 --> 00:01:14.444
Claim: F(k) is at most k squared

00:01:14.444 --> 00:01:16.260
for every k from 1.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.222
A program checks k = 1, 2, 3

00:01:20.222 --> 00:01:21.168
and so on.

00:01:21.168 --> 00:01:24.006
For a long time the claim holds.

00:01:24.006 --> 00:01:26.156
F(10) is 89

00:01:26.156 --> 00:01:27.962
at most 100.

00:01:28.048 --> 00:01:30.628
But F(11) is 144

00:01:30.714 --> 00:01:32.004
and forty-four

00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:35.272
more than 121.

00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:36.992
The claim is false

00:01:36.992 --> 00:01:40.690
and k = 11 is the counterexample.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.605
10 successes proved nothing

00:01:43.605 --> 00:01:46.612
1 failure settles the question.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.796
In fact, F(k) grows like 1.618

00:01:49.884 --> 00:01:51.919
six one eight to the k

00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:54.660
and any exponential eventually

00:01:54.749 --> 00:01:56.960
overtakes any power of k.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.483
Another one:

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:02.490
n^2 + n + 41 is prime

00:02:02.587 --> 00:02:06.203
for every n from 0 to 39.

00:02:06.301 --> 00:02:09.428
But n = 40 gives

00:02:09.526 --> 00:02:11.774
1681,

00:02:11.774 --> 00:02:14.510
which is 41 squared.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.724
Proof by contradiction

00:02:16.724 --> 00:02:18.288
to prove a statement

00:02:18.288 --> 00:02:20.064
assume that it is false

00:02:20.136 --> 00:02:22.623
and show that this assumption leads

00:02:22.694 --> 00:02:24.399
to something impossible.

00:02:24.399 --> 00:02:26.602
Then the assumption was wrong

00:02:26.602 --> 00:02:28.450
so the statement is true.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.312
Euclid's theorem

00:02:30.312 --> 00:02:33.335
there are infinitely many primes.

00:02:33.335 --> 00:02:37.159
Suppose not: there are only finitely many

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:38.671
p1 up to pn.

00:02:38.671 --> 00:02:42.228
Multiply them all together and add 1

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.917
call the result P.

00:02:44.006 --> 00:02:46.763
Dividing P by any of the primes

00:02:46.852 --> 00:02:48.719
leaves remainder 1

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:51.120
so none of them divides P.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.469
But P is greater than 1

00:02:53.546 --> 00:02:56.160
and every integer greater than 1

00:02:56.237 --> 00:02:58.544
has at least one prime factor.

00:02:58.621 --> 00:03:00.466
That prime factor is not

00:03:00.543 --> 00:03:02.158
on our complete list.

00:03:02.158 --> 00:03:03.465
A contradiction

00:03:03.465 --> 00:03:06.310
so there are infinitely many primes.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.412
Careful: P itself need not be prime.

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:11.781
A program multiplies the first

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.387
primes and adds 1.

00:03:13.387 --> 00:03:16.215
Up to 11, every result is prime.

00:03:16.291 --> 00:03:19.043
But 2 * 3 * 5 *

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.483
7 * 11 * 13 + 1

00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:25.158
is 30031

00:03:25.235 --> 00:03:27.146
which is 59 *

00:03:27.222 --> 00:03:28.904
509.

00:03:28.904 --> 00:03:29.745
Not prime

00:03:29.821 --> 00:03:32.879
but 59 and 509

00:03:32.955 --> 00:03:35.860
are both primes missing from the list.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.290
Induction proves a statement P(n) for

00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:44.436
every n from some starting value, n0.

00:03:44.436 --> 00:03:46.410
It has three parts.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.162
The basis: show P(n0) directly.

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:52.728
The induction hypothesis

00:03:52.728 --> 00:03:55.887
assume P(n) holds for some n.

00:03:55.887 --> 00:03:58.256
And the inductive step

00:03:58.256 --> 00:04:02.600
using the hypothesis, show P(n+1).

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.893
Think of a row of dominoes.

00:04:04.893 --> 00:04:07.555
The basis pushes the first one over.

00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:10.073
The inductive step guarantees that

00:04:10.145 --> 00:04:12.519
each domino knocks over the next

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:14.030
so all of them fall.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.289
Example: 1 + 2, up to n

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.761
equals n * (n + 1) / 2.

00:04:20.761 --> 00:04:24.984
The basis, n = 1: both sides are 1.

00:04:24.984 --> 00:04:27.893
Assume the formula for some n.

00:04:27.893 --> 00:04:30.802
The new sum, up to n + 1

00:04:30.802 --> 00:04:32.867
contains the old sum.

00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:36.527
Replace the old sum by the hypothesis

00:04:36.527 --> 00:04:39.717
and the algebra gives (n + 1)

00:04:39.717 --> 00:04:42.345
* (n + 2) / 2,

00:04:42.345 --> 00:04:46.380
the formula with n + 1 in place of n.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.002
That is the only creative move:

00:04:49.002 --> 00:04:51.713
find the old sum inside the new one.

00:04:51.786 --> 00:04:54.791
Induction is also how we will prove loops

00:04:54.864 --> 00:04:57.062
and recursive methods correct:

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:59.114
the basis is the base case

00:04:59.114 --> 00:05:00.506
and the step says

00:05:00.506 --> 00:05:02.704
if the smaller call is right,

00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:04.170
this call is right.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.794
Many data structures are

00:05:06.879 --> 00:05:08.582
defined recursively.

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:11.222
Here a binary tree is either a

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:14.458
single node with no children, a leaf,

00:05:14.458 --> 00:05:17.609
or a node with exactly two children,

00:05:17.609 --> 00:05:19.908
both of them binary trees.

00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:22.122
A node that is not a leaf

00:05:22.207 --> 00:05:23.910
is an internal node.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:27.412
Claim: every such tree with n leaves has

00:05:27.491 --> 00:05:29.618
n - 1 internal nodes.

00:05:29.697 --> 00:05:32.297
A program in the notes grows such

00:05:32.376 --> 00:05:34.346
trees from a single leaf

00:05:34.425 --> 00:05:37.104
by turning a leaf into an internal

00:05:37.183 --> 00:05:39.153
node with two new leaves.

00:05:39.153 --> 00:05:41.753
Each time, it prints the counts:

00:05:41.753 --> 00:05:44.668
three leaves and two internal nodes,

00:05:44.668 --> 00:05:48.214
four and three, five and four, six and five.

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.209
Each split loses one leaf, gains two,

00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:53.415
and adds one internal node,

00:05:53.415 --> 00:05:55.700
so the difference stays one.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.970
The proof is by strong induction

00:05:59.054 --> 00:06:01.072
on the number of leaves.

00:06:01.072 --> 00:06:01.660
Basis:

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.939
a tree with one leaf is a single node

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:06.956
with no internal nodes.

00:06:06.956 --> 00:06:07.461
Step:

00:06:07.461 --> 00:06:11.160
a bigger tree has a root with two subtrees,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.943
T1 and T2, each with at most n leaves.

00:06:14.943 --> 00:06:18.221
Its internal nodes are those of T1,

00:06:18.221 --> 00:06:20.743
those of T2, and the root.

00:06:20.743 --> 00:06:22.341
By the hypothesis,

00:06:22.341 --> 00:06:25.619
that is, the leaves of T1 - 1

00:06:25.619 --> 00:06:28.646
plus the leaves of T2 - 1

00:06:28.646 --> 00:06:31.840
plus 1: the leaves of T - 1.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.159
The Java methods have the

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:35.898
same shape as the proof

00:06:35.898 --> 00:06:37.637
A leaf is the base case,

00:06:37.707 --> 00:06:40.003
and an internal node combines the

00:06:40.072 --> 00:06:42.020
answers of its two subtrees.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.276
Now here is a proof that every

00:06:45.365 --> 00:06:47.684
horse has the same colour.

00:06:47.684 --> 00:06:51.252
Let P(n) say: in any set of n horses,

00:06:51.252 --> 00:06:54.553
all the horses have the same colour.

00:06:54.553 --> 00:06:57.853
Basis: a set of 1 horse is clearly

00:06:57.942 --> 00:06:59.280
all 1 colour.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.142
Step: take n + 1 horses

00:07:02.142 --> 00:07:05.433
and number them 1 up to n + 1.

00:07:05.433 --> 00:07:08.465
The first n horses are 1 colour

00:07:08.465 --> 00:07:10.110
by the hypothesis.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.756
So are the last n.

00:07:11.756 --> 00:07:14.181
The two sets share a horse,

00:07:14.181 --> 00:07:17.039
so the two colours are the same

00:07:17.126 --> 00:07:19.291
and all n + 1 horses

00:07:19.378 --> 00:07:21.110
are the same colour.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.932
Every sentence sounds reasonable,

00:07:23.932 --> 00:07:26.180
yet the conclusion is absurd.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.382
Where is the mistake?

00:07:28.459 --> 00:07:30.468
Find the first n for which

00:07:30.545 --> 00:07:32.476
the inductive step fails.

00:07:32.476 --> 00:07:35.720
Pause the video and look at the two sets.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.195
The step needs a horse that is in both sets:

00:07:43.195 --> 00:07:44.965
horses 2 up to n.

00:07:44.965 --> 00:07:48.417
Those exist only if n is at least 2.

00:07:48.417 --> 00:07:50.719
Go from 1 horse to 2,

00:07:50.719 --> 00:07:54.525
the two sets are horse 1, and horse 2.

00:07:54.525 --> 00:07:56.384
They do not overlap,

00:07:56.472 --> 00:07:59.748
and nothing links the colour of horse

00:07:59.836 --> 00:08:02.580
1 to the colour of horse 2.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.614
So the chain of dominoes breaks

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:07.692
at the very first step

00:08:07.692 --> 00:08:09.684
from 1 horse to 2.

00:08:09.684 --> 00:08:13.494
The step does work for every n from 2 on,

00:08:13.494 --> 00:08:15.312
but that is useless

00:08:15.312 --> 00:08:17.650
because P(2) is false.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.103
The lesson: the inductive step must work

00:08:21.181 --> 00:08:23.276
for every n from n0 on,

00:08:23.276 --> 00:08:25.138
including the smallest,

00:08:25.138 --> 00:08:27.930
so check the first step explicitly.

00:08:28.008 --> 00:08:31.111
A recursive method fails in the same way

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:34.292
when its recursive case silently assumes

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.240
that there are at least 2 elements.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:38.593
To sum up.

00:08:38.593 --> 00:08:41.452
One: a single failing case disproves

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:42.766
a for-all claim

00:08:42.766 --> 00:08:45.934
like F of 11 against 11 squared.

00:08:45.934 --> 00:08:48.870
Two: a proof by contradiction assumes

00:08:48.947 --> 00:08:51.575
the statement is false and derives

00:08:51.652 --> 00:08:53.275
something impossible

00:08:53.275 --> 00:08:55.593
as Euclid did for the primes.

00:08:55.593 --> 00:08:58.838
Three: induction needs a basis and a step

00:08:58.916 --> 00:09:01.466
that works from the very first n.

00:09:01.543 --> 00:09:04.170
Structural induction does the same

00:09:04.247 --> 00:09:06.179
for recursive structures,

00:09:06.179 --> 00:09:08.420
from the parts to the whole.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:12.160
Try this: disprove the claim that 2 to

00:09:12.245 --> 00:09:15.041
the n is greater than n cubed for

00:09:15.126 --> 00:09:17.245
every positive integer n.

00:09:17.245 --> 00:09:18.770
Which n break it?

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.181
In part three,

00:09:20.181 --> 00:09:23.289
we turn to the vocabulary of the course:

00:09:23.289 --> 00:09:26.320
types, data types and data abstraction.

